            "text": "There is a particular kind of silence that belongs only to early morning kitchens. The refrigerator hums like a distant ship. A spoon touches a mug with the small bright sound of something beginning. Outside, the world has not yet decided how urgently it needs to be taken seriously today.\n\nI like imagining that ordinary objects enjoy these hours. The chairs relax their posture. The clock stops pretending it understands time. Bread waits patiently in its paper bag, confident that being bread is already a respectable accomplishment. Nothing is improving itself. Nothing is building a personal brand before the sunrise.\n\nPerhaps we could learn something from this unambitious assembly. Not everything needs to become a lesson, of course; even that would be another task imposed upon the morning. But there is relief in noticing how much of life simply happens without applause: water warming, light arriving, dust crossing a sunbeam.\n\nLater, there will be messages to answer and decisions wearing the disguises of emergencies. Someone will ask what you have accomplished. You may have an answer, or you may only remember standing barefoot beside the counter, watching steam rise as though the cup were quietly thinking about becoming a cloud.\n\nThat memory counts for something. A life is not only its milestones, those large stones we arrange along the road to prove we traveled. It is also the roadside grass, the missed turn, the unexpected view. Sometimes the best part is merely being here before the day puts on its shoes."
          }
        ],
